[Schumacher–Westmoreland](https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.2929) and [Abramsky–Brandenburger](https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.0264) already establish the broader conceptual setting. [De Beaudrap](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1405.7381) is particularly important: its generic state convention imposes primitivity, whereas the nonprimitive separator here relies on retaining states such as π^a I. This is a real contract difference, not proof of historical novelty. The chain-ring module literature remains a substantial priority risk; the source inspected here establishes that background area, not that no equivalent orbit or filtering theorem exists.

## Mathematical scrutiny and fresh evidence

The residue-hyperplane argument needs coverage of **all lifts** outside the selected hyperplane; checking only residue directions would lose the ring-support conclusion. The Hardy construction needs its negative coefficient outside characteristic two. The reduction to leading layers uses a unit coordinate to control radical cancellation; nonzero and nonzero residue are not interchangeable. In the coding argument, independent residue vectors must lift to a basis. “Dividing by π” means choosing preimages in an ideal, not inverting a nilpotent.

The raw-teleportation necessity is credible because a nonzero singular branch cannot recover every input: a kernel collision defeats universal reconstruction. Sufficiency requires a complete analyzer and invertible correction branches, not just a nonzero contraction. These points deserve an independent proof review.

The fresh checker enumerated support assignments directly for all 12 unordered bases of the six primitive projective rays over Z/4. It did not use the Smith trichotomy to decide assignments. Exponents (0,2) and (1,2) were local; (0,1) was logical but not strong; (0,0) and (1,1) were strong, where exponent 2 denotes zero. This supports five small instances and catches some contract errors. It does not prove the arbitrary-ring theorem. Earlier favorable gate reports are useful inputs, not independent external certification.

## Extensions with a real research question

1. **Restricted measurement families.** Determine the smallest or cheapest allowed setting family that witnesses each Smith class, under a specified residue field and dimension. The current two-setting F2 obstruction already supplies one boundary. Success would be matching upper/lower bounds or a structural characterization beyond known field examples; merely rediscovering three-setting mobit contextuality is insufficient.
2. **Restricted encoder groups.** Replace all invertible encoders by a motivated gate family and classify reachable codebook spans. The unrestricted d h theorem is the baseline. A useful contribution would expose a gate-resource tradeoff, not repackage the same orbit under another basis.
3. **One controlled non-chain ring.** Start with a small ring whose ideals are not linearly ordered and identify exactly why the Smith invariant ceases to suffice. Seek a replacement invariant or a decisive counterexample family. Do not assume the chain-ring proof generalizes.

## Practical gate

Prioritize proof and matched-contract literature review over extending the scope. Keep the static model explicit and present it as an algebraic resource theory, without implying a physical realization or a computational speedup. If the separate ingredients prove classical, the unified separation can still support a carefully positioned technical article, provided it teaches something not already transparently contained in one source.

